==Calculating Stats==
:''Stat calculation is non-trivial and is best done by computer. While this section will try to be as accurate as possible, it may sometimes be more important to just remember a few simple rules that are accurate in most cases.''

Each Cat has an individual value for their base Health and Attack Power. This Initial Stat is used to determine the value of the cat's Health and Attack Power based on their current level.

A level 1 [[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Bahamut Cat]] has 1,500 HP and 5,000 damage. When upgrading it to level 2, it will gain 20% of its initial stats, so it will have 1,800 HP and 6,000 damage. At level 3, it will have 2,100 HP and 7,000 damage. At level <math>n</math>, you could expect Bahamut to have

<math>\text{Stats at level }n = \text{Initial Stat} + (n - 1) \times (\text{Initial Stat} \times 20\%)</math>

For example, at level 4, Bahamut would have <math>1500 + (3 \times 1500 \times 20\%) = 1500 + 900 = 2400</math> HP and <math>5000 + (3 \times 5000 \times 20\%) = 5000 + 3000 = 8000</math> damage.

Due to how stats are represented in game, this calculation must yield an integer, and so the actual calculation would also apply the <math>\operatorname{round}()</math> function to the result.

A neater, equivalent way of writing this calculation is to factor out the initial stat, giving

<math>\text{Stats at level }n = \operatorname{round}\left(\text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{n+4}{5} \right)</math>

===Treasure Bonuses===
When the [[Main Chapters Levels#Legendary Cat Shield|Legendary Cat Shield]] or [[Main Chapters Levels#Legendary Cat Sword|Legendary Cat Sword]] are activated, their effect gives a direct increase to the value of the corresponding initial HP/attack power stat. This effect is independent of the growth rate calculation, which is used to determine a cat's health and damage at their current level.

With 100% Treasures from one chapter of Empire of Cats, the relevant stats will be multiplied by 1.5. With 100% Treasures from all 3 chapters, the relevant stats will be multiplied by 2.5. Therefore, taking into account Treasure bonuses, the cat's stats at level n will be:

<math>\text{Stats at level }n = \Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}\left(\text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{n+4}{5} \right) \times \text{TB} \Bigr\rfloor</math>
*TB = Treasure Bonus Multiplier = 1.5, 2.0 or 2.5 at 100%, 200% or 300% respectively
*The symbols ⌊ ⌋ represent the floor function, which essentially removes any decimals from the number between them. For example, <math>\lfloor2.99\rfloor = 2</math>.

:''For most cats, at level 10 they will have 7x their initial stats, 12x at level 20, 17x at 30, 22x at 40, 27x at 50 and 32x at 60. Depending on stat scaling (see below), these numbers may be wildly different. The numbers may also be slightly (and by slightly we mean at most 2, which is almost always negligible) different if one of the initial stats is not a multiple of 5.''

===Higher-Level Cats and Level Reduction Points===
The above formula will work for most cats at a low level, but will not be accurate at higher levels: the specific mechanism for how levels scale is variable every 10 levels rather than a flat 20%. For example, [[Gacha Cat (Special Cat)|Gacha Cat]]'s levels scale as the following:

{| class="article-table"
! Level || 1-20 || 21-30 || 31-40 || 41-50 || 51-60 || 61+
|-
! Increase || 20% || 60% || 120% || 180% || 20% || 10%
|}

Until level 20, its stats will align with the above formulae given, so with all EoC Treasures at level 20 its stats would be:

<math>\Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}\left(\text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{\text{Level}+4}{100 \div \text{Scaling}} \right) \times \text{TB} \Bigr\rfloor</math>

Gacha Cat's base HP is 1,500 and its base attack is 1. Substituting in these stats, as well as 20 for level, 20 for scaling (as per the previous table) and 2.5 for TB, this is:

<math>\Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}\left(\text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{24}{5} \right) \times 2.5 \Bigr\rfloor = 18,000\text{ HP}, 12\text{ AP}</math>

However, this changes for the next 10 levels, so when upgrading to level 21 it will now receive a 60% boost in stats (as with the boosts up to level 20, this 60% gets applied before the value is rounded), so the calculation will now be:

<math>\Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}\left(\text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{24}{5} + \text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{\text{Levels after 20}}{100 \div \text{Scaling}} \right) \times \text{TB} \Bigr\rfloor</math>

Substituting in appropriate values, this is

<math>\Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}\left(\text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{24}{5} + \text{Initial Stat} \times \frac{1}{100 \div 60} \right) \times 2.5 \Bigr\rfloor</math>

<math>\Bigl\lfloor \text{round}\left(1,500 \times \frac{24}{5} + 1,500 \times \frac{1}{100 \div 60} \right) \times 2.5 \Bigr\rfloor = 20,250\text{ HP}</math>

<math>\Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}\left(1 \times \frac{24}{5} + 1 \times \frac{1}{100 \div 60} \right) \times 2.5 \Bigr\rfloor = \Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}(5.4) \times 2.5 \Bigr\rfloor = \lfloor 5 \times 2.5 \rfloor = 12\text{ AP}</math>

Therefore, at level 21 with all Treasures, Gacha cat will have 20,250 HP and 12 AP.

At level 50, Gacha Cat's stats would be as follows:

<math>\Bigl\lfloor \operatorname{round}\left(\text{Initial} \times \left(\frac{20 + 4}{100 \div 20} + \frac{10}{100 \div 60} + \frac{10}{100 \div 120} + \frac{10}{100 \div 180} \right)\right) \times 2.5 \Bigr\rfloor = 153,000\text{ HP}, 102\text{ AP}</math>

====Reduction Points====
:''Reduction points are an outdated method of calculating stats, but for most cats they are still accurate, and using reduction points to calculate stats is much easier than the actual calculation.''
Certain cats like Gacha Cat, [[Principal Cat (Special Cat)|Principal Cat]] and [[Choco Capsule (Special Cat)|Choco Capsule]] are special cases of cats with level scaling where stats increase. For virtually every other cat, a much simpler way of reasoning about a cat's stats at a certain level is to use a "reduction point", where the scaling of the cat's stats goes down from 20% to 10% of the initial stats. Some cats will also have a second reduction point that goes down to 5% of initial stats, as detailed below.

